WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Successful React Native upgrades require a structured migration,

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Diff, Apply, Validate, and Repeat.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.000
The developers who get this right treat the process as a planned refactor,

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.000
rather than a simple dependency bump.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
To keep things stable, we rely on a strict four-phase strategy

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
that ensures every change is isolated and verified.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
This flow chart illustrates the golden rule of the upgrade path.

8
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Minor version bumps, like moving from 0.74 to 0.75,

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.000
require mandatory sequential stepping stones.

10
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
You must take them one minor at a time.

11
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
But for patch upgrades, like 0.76.0 to 0.76.5,

12
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.000
it's safe to collapse fixes into a single jump.

13
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Moving forward, 0.74 is a critical stop,

14
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.000
introducing the turbo-module interop layer.

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.000
By passing it, guarantees failures transitioning to the new architecture.

16
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Jumping multiple minor versions at once makes it impossible to isolate which change broke what.

17
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Sticking to a strict sequential path guarantees your upgrade remains deterministic and easy to debug.

18
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Phase 1 begins before you modify any code.

19
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
The most expensive mistakes happen when you begin applying Diff's

20
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
without first understanding the specific release landscape.

21
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.000
This is the official React Native change log.

22
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.000
First, scan specifically for deprecated APIs and breaking changes requiring manual code adjustments.

23
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Next, check the React Native releases discussion board.

24
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
It surfaces known issues caught during the release candidate cycle,

25
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
not included in the primary change log it.

26
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Pin pointing these native only behavior changes in the documentation

27
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.000
prevents you from blind debugging issues that will never appear in a standard JavaScript code diff.

28
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Phase 2 builds a blueprint using the React Native upgrade helper.

29
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
This essential tool generates a clean native code diff between current and target versions.

30
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.000
The tool works by running a new project initialization at both versions

31
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
and comparing the raw template outputs to show exactly what changed.

32
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.000
You will work through these file changes from top to bottom, beginning with low risk items,

33
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.000
like standard version bumps in your package.json.

34
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
This chart categorizes the risk level of different project files.

35
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
While the package.json is generally straightforward,

36
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.000
files like your Android build.gradle and iOS app delegate move into the high-risk zone.

37
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Errors in these files lead to immediate build failures or boot crashes.

38
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.000
The resulting diff serves as a customized blueprint for the native adjustments required,

39
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
eliminating guesswork from the framework transition.

40
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Phase 3 is the application of those changes.

41
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.000
While automated upgrade commands are available, they frequently create irresolvable conflicts

42
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.000
in heavily modified applications.

43
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.000
This comparison shows a generic template next to a customized local file.

44
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Avoid the copy-paste mistake.

45
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Overwriting your file entirely will erase your unique configurations.

46
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Instead, you must map the template changes manually onto your local files at the specific property level.

47
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.000
In this snippet, we update just a single integer,

48
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.000
changing the target SDK version from 34 to 35,

49
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.000
while leaving the surrounding custom code untouched.

50
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.000
Manual property level mapping is the only way to absorb core framework updates

51
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.000
while protecting any undocumented native integrations from being erased.

52
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Phase 4 involves a final audit of your applied code against the breaking changes checklist you created in Phase 1.

53
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Before you attempt the first post-upgrade compile,

54
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.000
you must perform one mandatory step.

55
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Entirely purge your local build cache.

56
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Compiling over old cached artifacts triggers fake, non-deterministic failures.

57
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Starting from an absolute clean state ensures your build results are completely accurate.

58
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
This clean state ensures that any resulting build errors are legitimate framework issues that require attention,

59
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000
rather than leftover artifact corruption.

60
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.000
Once the application compiles successfully,

61
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.000
you are ready to launch and begin final validation.

62
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.000
For the initial smoke test, you must follow a strict hardware rule.

63
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Use a physical, low-end device rather than a simulator.

64
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Flagship devices possess enough raw processing power

65
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.000
to completely mask subtle performance regressions that might have been introduced by the new framework version.

66
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.000
If the application performs smoothly on this older hardware, lock in the validated version.

67
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.000
You are now ready to repeat this cycle for the next minor bump.

68
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
By treating upgrades as structured, incremental, and rigorously validated refactors,

69
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.000
you ensure a stable progression toward the new architecture.

